A||||23||CUTTER||87GT sways above 60mph......why?||||1||
Z||000000||CUTTER||05-29-2000||03:39 PM||||The front end sways back and forth above 60-65mph somewhere around there almost like a boat. The steering wheel doesnt move or anything. What could it be? usually it only does it when I slam the gas and start going above 60-65 when the front is lighter from getting on the gas. But when It starts to sway I get off the gas and it stops. It almost feels like a tire is loose but its not, I just got new wheel bearings a few days ago along with new break pads. What could this sway problem be and what should I check?<BR>Thanks, Shane||||reg||1
Z||000001||CUTTER||05-29-2000||03:42 PM||||Oh yeah by the way, I do have a jiggly steering wheel, the shop said it just needs some screws tightnend. Could this be my "swaying" problem?<BR>Thanks, Shane||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||05-29-2000||06:03 PM||||Here's a list of things to try:<BR> Tire air pressure, Wheel weights (did one fall off?) ball joints, tie rod ends, Tires worn since last alignment, 4-wheel alignment may be off if you've hit a pothole or bumped a curb, and finally a bad tire (sometimes the belt inside the tire can seperate and cause all kinds of problems). ||||reg||1
Z||000003||CUTTER||05-29-2000||06:10 PM||||well, I just had alighnment done when I got my new tires put on. I got new wheel bearings and thought that would fix the problem but it didnt. The tire pressure should be good since the new tires are only 1 week old. How do i check tie rods and ball joints?||||reg||1
Z||000004||N_Man||05-29-2000||09:43 PM||||you might want to jack the front of the car up and try wiggling the tire's at the 3 and 9 position.<BR>If you feel any play get a helper to inspect the tie rods and ball joints well you wiggle. This way you stay claen  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR> <BR>The passenger side rack bushing has also been known to go bad, if everything else is in good shape this may be the ticket.  Rodney sells replacements.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GT||05-29-2000||09:47 PM||||Cutter, I have the same problem.  It really annoys me too.  I had new tires and an alignment done about 6 months ago and it still does it.  I also had the tie rods replaced and ball joints checked out, which were said to be in good shape.  It baffles me too.  Im almost afraid to pass anyone on a 2 lane when I am over 55 mph!  Its ridiculous. Somebody suggest something that could help us out. :-)||||reg||1
Z||000006||CUTTER||05-29-2000||10:28 PM||||Hey 87GT when you find out what it is tell me, and if I find out what it is I will tell you! It really makes me mad, I lost to a honda civic because of it.<P>Shane, blue87GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||Sillhouette.com||05-29-2000||10:38 PM||||My 87 GT only behaved like that when the tire pressures were not proper.  Since you just changed your rubber it's possible that it does not seal properly so I would blame it on that first.  Next it's maybe that funky shock absorber on the steering rack.<P>Hope this helps||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||05-30-2000||12:27 AM||||If it's really the front....... crappy craddle bushings will make the car float too. steering stabilizers on the other hand rarely account for floaty steering.<P>steering wheel wobble should be fixed immediatly. DO NOT drive a car with the column messed up. The whole top of the column can come loose very suddenly unless all <B>4</B> of the tilt unit screws are tight.<P>repairing the tilt unit properly takes a good hour and a half just to do the 4 screws. YOU MUST do all 4. DO NOT take some peoples advice and only do the 2 that are easy to reach. doing only the easy 2 leaves the column seriously weakened.<P>follow the cave link at the bottom of this page for more details. When ever I do this job I check all the things in that article about this problem and a dozen more things at least.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||CUTTER||05-30-2000||02:54 AM||||Well I checked my tire pressure and its 34 all the way around and the max tire pressure for my tires is 35 so its not my tires. It acually wasnt doing it that bad tonight. I did notice when i was on the highway when I slamed the gas the front comes up a little more then it should, and when I let off it sort of comes down on the right side, like i see my headlights(the light from the headlights) go up when I slam the gas and when i let off you can feel the car go down a little more on the right side, you see the headlights come down on sort of a angle, the past few days when I was going around turns around 35-40mph it was even felling floaty. How do I check the wheel weights?||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||05-30-2000||09:21 AM||||definatly check the cradle bushings.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fldevel||05-30-2000||12:52 PM||||i have 2 85 GTs and i get alot of sway in the rear end over 80 mph ..seems like the rear is trying to outrun the front end ...but sence both 85 GTs do it i guess its normal ...just dont make any sudden sharp turns  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||lennyb4||05-30-2000||01:04 PM||||What size tires did you just put on? An oversized tire could cause that effect to. ( i found this out the hard way)It would allow to much updraft to be generated in the front (and rear too) causing swaying issues.||||reg||1
Z||000013||peterh||05-30-2000||02:21 PM||||I rebuilt the front end on my 86SE, V6 , auto with new upper & lower poly bushings, new upper & lower ball joints, new kyb shocks, rebuilt rack and new tires up front. My car is also kind of wobble at higher speeds (45mph & up). I have not done anything to the rear suspesnsion, etc. because I was waiting until my engine died and I would be forced to put in a worked 3.4l. How hard is it to replace cradle bushings, etc???<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||87GT||05-30-2000||07:30 PM||||Ogre, I kind of thought the cradle bushings played a role in this.  Ill have to check that out and see if it needs to be done.  I would think it does. thanks for the info.||||reg||1
Z||000015||88formula||05-30-2000||09:45 PM||||Could be a loose cradle bolt or worn out struts and shocks. Does the car keep bouncing over bumps? If it does your struts and shocks are shot. I would definitely check your cradle bolts though. They could be loose or worse rotted.||||reg||1
Z||000016||CUTTER||05-31-2000||01:14 AM||||I would check them but tell you the truth I have no clue what cradle bushings are, what they do or anything. I have no clue where the bolts are to check. Where are they? My suspension is worn, and I plan on getting performance suspension sometime soon in the near future, but for now can anyone tell me where the bolts are located?<P>Thanks, Shane||||reg||5
Z||000017||fieroshop||05-31-2000||10:49 AM||||Hey guys all of you have the same problem, infact my wife's 86 GT has the problem and so do all my freinds that have an 87 on back. The problem is you have bumpsteer and something can be done about it. Check with Ryan motorsports and the have a kit to eliminate this problem.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Alex4mula||05-31-2000||03:23 PM||||My 88 Formula have had that problem several times. Causes has been bad bearings, bad alignment, loose axle-hub nuts and loose suspension components. On pre 88s I would be very suspicious of those cradle bushings. After 10-16 years they should get worn and screw up the alignment under torque most noticeable at high speed. Find out and fix it. It could be very dangerous...||||reg||1
Z||000019||jhogans||05-31-2000||04:58 PM||||I remember posting this same issue I had with my Fiero several months ago.  I still cannot figure out the problem.  Hopefully the suggestions I see here will help me out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000020||batboy||05-31-2000||05:43 PM||||Cutter, the cradle is a sub-frame support that the engine, tranny, and rear suspension attaches to. It is bolted onto the main space frame using 4 big bolts and rubber bushings. If you jack the rear end way up and crawl under the rear of the car, I think you will see what I mean. Look at the bushings, if they look mushy, deformed, or have splits...then they need replaced. To replace them, you need to carefully support the cradle, there's a lot of weight on that thing. I'm going to try and loosen all the bolts up, but only remove and replace them one or two at a time. Lining the bolt holes back up can be a challenge if the cradle shifts a little.||||reg||1
Z||000021||lowCG||05-31-2000||05:51 PM||||The stock rear springs are pretty soft right at the first part of the travel,not that I think this alone would do the sway/float thing,but would make any slop in front-end parts(lower balljoints,wheel bearings) seem worse when the gas is applied.<BR>Also try and move the front wheels by grabbing the top of the tire while on the ground and moving in and out.The parts in question are more likely to be in the worn portion of their travel like this than when up in the air.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Will||06-02-2000||03:32 PM||||My '86 SE V6 does this. I KNOW it's because the rear shocks are dead. One is not as dead as the other. This causes one side to move up and down faster than the other. This, in conjunction with the rear bump steer causes the car to sway and be uncertain in a straight line on bumpy roads or after hard bumps at speed.<P>     I engineered a Fiero front swaybar onto the back. This helped the problem immensely.<BR>     The best way to get rid of this is to eliminate bump steer and get High Quality struts (possibly coil over)<BR>     Try Ryane Motorsports &lt;www.ryanemotorsports.com&gt; and their bump steer kit &lt;http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/bump.htm&gt;||||reg||1
Z||000023||JohnnyK||06-04-2000||01:08 PM||||Probably not your problem, but remember it did this one mine and it was due to the engine cradle snapping/rusting where the a-arm attached to it.. (Mine did it at lower speeds, but the break is REALLY bad)||||reg||1
